86	\subsection{Quality-Diversity Non-Tradeoff}
87	\label{sec:quality_diversity}
88	
89	Curation produces neither a quality gain large enough to motivate its use nor a diversity cost large enough to discourage it. Mean ArmoRM scores at Gen~2 range from 0.1096 (C5) to 0.1143 (C2), a spread of less than 5\%. All conditions show mild quality decreases from Gen~0 to Gen~2 ($-$0.3\% to $-$1.3\%). The absence of meaningful quality improvement reflects saturation in the candidate quality distribution: the score gap between top-1 and average candidate is approximately 0.04, too narrow for the fine-tuned model to learn a meaningfully different quality level. The combined picture is that reward-based curation operates in a narrow effect regime at this scale: it does not collapse diversity, does not measurably improve quality, and its relative diversity effect depends more on training capacity than on curation strategy.
